<<<
It is by no means uncommon for Í very ordinary words to remain Í latent for long periods. ` To take Í only some notable cases in the letter | B, the OED records such disappearf ances of balsam (600 years), bloom | (the iron-foundry word; 600 years), | bosk (500 years),
===
It is by no means uncommon for very ordinary words to remain latent for long periods. To take only some notable cases in the letter B, the OED records such disappearances of *balsam* (600 years), *bloom* (the iron-foundry word; 600 years), *bosk* (500 years),
>>>

<<<
braze (to make of Í brass; 550 years); but the reap- | pearance of these, except perhaps | of bosk, was not so much a deliberate | revival as a re-emergence out of the | obscurity of talk into the light of | literature. It is only with deliberate { revivals, however, that it is worth Í while to concern ourselves here—
===
*braze* (to make of brass; 550 years); but the reappearance of these, except perhaps of *bosk*, was not so much a deliberate revival as a re-emergence out of the obscurity of talk into the light of literature. It is only with deliberate revivals, however, that it is worth while to concern ourselves here—
>>>

